[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Criteria API for Dynamic Type-Safe Queries in JPA"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Java Persistence API (JPA) Criteria API provides a programmatic way to build database queries. Unlike JPQL or native SQL strings, which are evaluated at runtime and prone to syntax errors, the Criteria API is type-safe and allows developers to construct queries dynamically using Java objects."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use the Criteria API, you need an EntityManager instance and a metamodel class if you want strict type safety. Let us assume we have an entity named 'Employee' with fields like 'id', 'name', 'department', and 'salary'."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Criteria Query Construction"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Building a basic query involves three main components: CriteriaBuilder, CriteriaQuery, and Root. Here is how you can retrieve all employees:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"CriteriaBuilder cb = entityManager.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<Employee> query = cb.createQuery(Employee.class);
Root<Employee> root = query.from(Employee.class);
query.select(root);
List<Employee> result = entityManager.createQuery(query).getResultList();"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Dynamic Queries with Predicates"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The true power of the Criteria API shines when building dynamic queries based on optional search criteria. You can collect predicates in a list and apply them conditionally."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"CriteriaBuilder cb = entityManager.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<Employee> query = cb.createQuery(Employee.class);
Root<Employee> root = query.from(Employee.class);

List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();

String nameFilter = request.getName();
if (nameFilter != null && !nameFilter.isEmpty()) {
    predicates.add(cb.like(root.get("name"), "%" + nameFilter + "%"));
}

Double minSalary = request.getMinSalary();
if (minSalary != null) {
    predicates.add(cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("salary"), minSalary));
}

query.where(cb.and(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0])));
List<Employee> employees = entityManager.createQuery(query).getResultList();"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Benefits of the Criteria API"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Type-Safety: Reduces syntax and spelling errors by avoiding raw strings."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dynamic Query Building: Easily append WHERE clauses and conditions based on runtime user input."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Refactoring Support: Integrated with IDE refactoring tools when using the JPA Metamodel."}]
